"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkvoix=globalThis.webpackChunkvoix||[]).push([[8130],{7735(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"importance-of-documentation","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/importance-of-documentation","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-02-27-importance-of-documentation.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-02-27-importance-of-documentation.md","title":"Why documentation is a product, not a checklist","description":"Documentation is often treated as a final step before release. That is usually","date":"2026-02-27T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Docs Strategy","permalink":"/blog/tags/docs-strategy","description":"Documentation planning tied to product and support outcomes."},{"inline":false,"label":"Docs Architecture","permalink":"/blog/tags/docs-architecture","description":"Platform and system design decisions for scalable documentation."}],"readingTime":0.86,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"voix Studio","title":"Portfolio studio","url":"https://voix.studio","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/voix"},"imageURL":"/img/logo.svg","key":"voix"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"importance-of-documentation","title":"Why documentation is a product, not a checklist","authors":["voix"],"tags":["docs-strategy","docs-architecture"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Is your documentation strategy ready for AI?","permalink":"/blog/is-your-docs-strategy-ready-for-ai"}},"content":"Documentation is often treated as a final step before release. That is usually\\nwhere teams lose adoption, speed, and trust.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nWhen docs are clear, current, and easy to navigate, users solve problems on\\ntheir own. They onboard faster, use more of your product, and open fewer\\nsupport tickets.\\n\\nWhen docs are outdated or scattered across tools, support becomes the fallback\\nfor basic questions. Engineering gets interrupted, launch confidence drops, and\\nteams spend time repeating answers instead of shipping improvements.\\n\\nStrong documentation does four jobs at once:\\n\\n- It reduces support load by answering frequent questions early\\n- It improves activation by making key workflows easier to complete\\n- It protects quality by keeping release notes, APIs, and guides in sync\\n- It preserves institutional knowledge as teams and products evolve\\n\\nTreating documentation like a product means giving it ownership, architecture,\\nand delivery workflows. Version control, review gates, and CI/CD publishing\\nmake documentation reliable in the same way they make code reliable.\\n\\nGood docs are not just helpful content. They are infrastructure for growth."},{"id":"is-your-docs-strategy-ready-for-ai","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/is-your-docs-strategy-ready-for-ai","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-02-27-is-your-docs-strategy-ready-for-ai.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-02-27-is-your-docs-strategy-ready-for-ai.md","title":"Is your documentation strategy ready for AI?","description":"Your documentation is one of your most strategic product assets. But in many","date":"2026-02-27T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Docs Strategy","permalink":"/blog/tags/docs-strategy","description":"Documentation planning tied to product and support outcomes."},{"inline":false,"label":"Docs Architecture","permalink":"/blog/tags/docs-architecture","description":"Platform and system design decisions for scalable documentation."},{"inline":false,"label":"Governance","permalink":"/blog/tags/governance","description":"Ownership, standards, and lifecycle controls for docs operations."}],"readingTime":2.75,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"voix Studio","title":"Portfolio studio","url":"https://voix.studio","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/voix"},"imageURL":"/img/logo.svg","key":"voix"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"is-your-docs-strategy-ready-for-ai","title":"Is your documentation strategy ready for AI?","authors":["voix"],"tags":["docs-strategy","docs-architecture","governance"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Why documentation is a product, not a checklist","permalink":"/blog/importance-of-documentation"},"nextItem":{"title":"Build an acting portfolio that gets you cast","permalink":"/blog/acting-portfolio-gets-you-cast"}},"content":"Your documentation is one of your most strategic product assets. But in many\\nteams, it is still scattered across legacy tools, shared drives, and isolated\\nportals that do not sync.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nThe challenge is no longer just writing content. The challenge is delivering\\naccurate knowledge, securely and at scale, to users, support teams, and AI\\nsystems.\\n\\nAt voix, we design documentation platforms that turn static files into governed,\\nAI-ready knowledge systems.\\n\\n## 1. Build a single source of truth\\n\\nWhen docs live in multiple tools, version drift is guaranteed. One portal\\nshows an old workflow while the updated process sits somewhere else.\\n\\nConsolidating docs into one governed source of truth solves this:\\n\\n- updates happen once, then flow everywhere\\n- teams work from the same approved version\\n- users stop seeing contradictory instructions\\n\\n## 2. Make content portable and integration-ready\\n\\nA modern documentation platform should not be a silo. It should connect to your\\nproduct, support, and learning ecosystems.\\n\\nThat means open formats, structured content, and API-friendly delivery so docs\\ncan be reused across portals, in-app help, chat assistants, and internal tools.\\n\\n## 3. Design for modular reuse\\n\\nTeams lose time rewriting the same instructions for different audiences.\\nComponent-level content architecture fixes that.\\n\\nInstead of duplicating content, you define reusable blocks for procedures,\\npolicies, and references, then assemble them into multiple outputs with clear\\nownership and lifecycle controls.\\n\\n## 4. Add metadata that AI can actually use\\n\\nAI search only works when content is structured and tagged properly.\\n\\nStrong metadata improves:\\n\\n- semantic retrieval\\n- faceted navigation\\n- relevance by role, region, and product area\\n- deduplication and content quality checks\\n\\nWithout architecture and tagging, AI produces noisy answers from partial or\\noutdated context.\\n\\n## 5. Automate publishing and governance\\n\\nManual uploads and ad hoc publishing create delays and errors.\\n\\nWith Docs-as-Code workflows, CI/CD pipelines, and quality gates, you can:\\n\\n- publish faster with fewer regressions\\n- enforce link, style, and structural checks\\n- maintain audit trails across every content change\\n\\n## 6. Measure usage, not just page views\\n\\nIf you only track page opens, you miss how documentation is actually used.\\n\\nOperational analytics help teams see where users drop off, what content is\\nreused, and which pages drive ticket deflection. That data makes content\\nstrategy measurable and improvable.\\n\\n## 7. Protect access with enterprise controls\\n\\nDocumentation must be accessible and secure at the same time.\\n\\nRole-based access, SSO integration, and environment-level controls let you\\ndeliver external and internal content safely without fragmenting your platform.\\n\\n## Final point\\n\\nAI readiness is not a plugin. It is a content architecture decision.\\n\\nTeams that invest in migration, structure, metadata, and governance now are the\\nones that will get reliable results from AI later.\\n\\n## Frequently asked questions\\n\\n### What changes first: tools or structure?\\n\\nStructure first. Tool choices are easier and safer once information types,\\nmetadata, and governance are defined.\\n\\n### Can we become AI-ready without rewriting everything?\\n\\nYes. Most teams can phase this work by prioritizing critical docs, adding\\nmetadata, and introducing templates and standards over time.\\n\\n### Do we need to replace our LMS or support portal?\\n\\nNot always. A well-designed documentation platform can integrate with existing\\nsystems while centralizing ownership and quality controls.\\n\\n### How do we avoid migration disruption?\\n\\nUse phased rollouts, redirect planning, automated validation, and contributor\\ntraining so the operating model is stable before full cutover."},{"id":"acting-portfolio-gets-you-cast","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/acting-portfolio-gets-you-cast","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-01-03-acting-portfolio.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-01-03-acting-portfolio.md","title":"Build an acting portfolio that gets you cast","description":"Ready to work? Start building your portfolio \u2192","date":"2026-01-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"portfolio","permalink":"/blog/tags/portfolio"},{"inline":true,"label":"acting","permalink":"/blog/tags/acting"},{"inline":true,"label":"process","permalink":"/blog/tags/process"}],"readingTime":3.46,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"voix Studio","title":"Portfolio studio","url":"https://voix.studio","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/voix"},"imageURL":"/img/logo.svg","key":"voix"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"acting-portfolio-gets-you-cast","title":"Build an acting portfolio that gets you cast","authors":["voix"],"tags":["portfolio","acting","process"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Is your documentation strategy ready for AI?","permalink":"/blog/is-your-docs-strategy-ready-for-ai"},"nextItem":{"title":"A practical docs migration playbook","permalink":"/blog/docs-migration-playbook"}},"content":"Ready to work? Start building your portfolio \u2192\\n\\nA resume lists what you did. A portfolio shows what you can do.\\n\\nFor casting directors and agents, your portfolio is the only thing that matters before you enter the room. It proves your range. It validates your talent.\\n\\nThis guide explains how to build a portfolio that works as hard as you do. You will learn to capture attention, define your brand, and own your online presence without paying monthly rent on your website.\\n\\nWhether you are a student or a working professional, this is how you set yourself apart.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The essentials\\n\\n1. Gather your materials. Get professional headshots, a clean resume, and a sharp showreel.\\n2. Write a clear bio. State your training and your type. Be honest.\\n3. Build on solid ground. Use open-source tools like voix. Pay once. Own the code.\\n4. Add social proof. Display reviews, awards, and press.\\n5. Stay current. Update your look and your credits regularly.\\n\\n## How to build your portfolio in 5 steps\\n\\nYour portfolio must be professional and personal. Follow these steps to build a site that tells the truth about your ability.\\n\\n### 1. Select your materials\\n\\nStart with the basics. Do not clutter the page. You need four things:\\n\\n- Headshots: Pick two or three images. One commercial (warm), one theatrical (serious), one neutral. Use good lighting. High resolution is mandatory.\\n- Resume: List your contact info, union status, training, and credits. Keep it detailed. If you have special skills (combat, dance, accents), list them here.\\n- Showreel: You have sixty seconds to impress a director. Keep your reel under two minutes. Put your best acting in the first ten seconds.\\n- Contact info: Make it easy to find you.\\n\\n### 2. Write an honest bio\\n\\nYour bio defines you. It is your introduction. Keep it to two paragraphs.\\n\\nCasting directors want to know the person behind the character. Mention your background, your training, and what drives you. If you studied at a specific conservatory or have a niche skill, write it down.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n> \\"I am a Chicago-based actor focused on gritty, realistic drama. I trained at The Second City and have appeared in independent features and regional theater. I bring intensity and focus to the work.\\"\\n\\n### 3. Design and own your platform\\n\\nMost portfolio builders rent you space. They charge you every month to keep your site alive.\\n\\nDon\'t rent. Own.\\n\\nUse an open-source builder like voix. You pay a single fee for the build. The code belongs to you. The only recurring cost is the domain name (the .com), which you pay directly to the registrar.\\n\\nYour site needs these pages:\\n\\n- Home/About: Your face and your bio.\\n- Media: Your showreel and voice clips.\\n- Gallery: Production stills and headshots.\\n- Resume: A viewable list and a downloadable PDF.\\n- Contact: Representation info and a direct email.\\n\\nDesign tip: Keep it clean. Use white space. Use black text. Do not use flashy animations. Let your acting be the loudest thing on the page.\\n\\n### 4. Prove your value\\n\\nAnyone can call themselves an actor. Prove it.\\n\\nAdd a section for press and achievements. This is social proof. It tells a director that others have hired you and liked the result.\\n\\n- Awards: Display logos or certificates.\\n- Reviews: Quote the best lines from reputable sources.\\n- Testimonials: Ask directors or coaches for a sentence about your work ethic.\\n\\nIf you are working on something now, list it. An active actor is a hireable actor.\\n\\n### 5. Keep it fresh\\n\\nA stale portfolio hurts your career.\\n\\nUpdate your headshots when you change your hair or age. Update your reel when you get better footage. If a credit on your resume is five years old and amateur, cut it.\\n\\nYour portfolio is a living document. Treat it that way.\\n\\n## Frequently asked questions\\n\\nHow long should my reel be? One to two minutes. Quality beats quantity. If the audio is bad, cut it.\\n\\nDo I need a printed portfolio? Yes. Digital gets you the audition. Print is for the room. Bring a headshot and resume stapled together.\\n\\nHow often should I update? Every six months. Or immediately after you finish a major project."},{"id":"docs-migration-playbook","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/docs-migration-playbook","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2025-11-07-docs-migration-playbook/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2025-11-07-docs-migration-playbook/index.md","title":"A practical docs migration playbook","description":"Most documentation migrations fail in the handover phase, not in content","date":"2025-11-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Docs Migration","permalink":"/blog/tags/docs-migration","description":"Moving documentation from proprietary tools to open-source stacks."},{"inline":false,"label":"Governance","permalink":"/blog/tags/governance","description":"Ownership, standards, and lifecycle controls for docs operations."}],"readingTime":0.53,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"voix Studio","title":"Portfolio studio","url":"https://voix.studio","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/voix"},"imageURL":"/img/logo.svg","key":"voix"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"docs-migration-playbook","title":"A practical docs migration playbook","authors":["voix"],"tags":["docs-migration","governance"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Build an acting portfolio that gets you cast","permalink":"/blog/acting-portfolio-gets-you-cast"},"nextItem":{"title":"Maintenance that keeps your voice current","permalink":"/blog/maintenance-that-works"}},"content":"Most documentation migrations fail in the handover phase, not in content\\nconversion.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nTeams can usually move content. The hard part is making sure the new system\\nstays healthy after launch.\\n\\nUse this playbook:\\n\\n- Define scope: what is being migrated now vs later\\n- Assign owners: who approves content and who maintains the platform\\n- Set quality gates: linting, broken-link checks, and content review\\n- Plan redirects: preserve URLs users and search engines already depend on\\n- Train contributors: new workflows fail if only one person understands them\\n\\nMigration is successful when the new platform is easier to run than the old\\none. That requires governance, not just scripts."},{"id":"maintenance-that-works","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/maintenance-that-works","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2025-08-26-welcome/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2025-08-26-welcome/index.md","title":"Maintenance that keeps your voice current","description":"A portfolio should not drift out of date. The annual care plan keeps your","date":"2025-08-26T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"maintenance","permalink":"/blog/tags/maintenance"},{"inline":true,"label":"portfolio","permalink":"/blog/tags/portfolio"}],"readingTime":0.25,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"voix Studio","title":"Portfolio studio","url":"https://voix.studio","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/voix"},"imageURL":"/img/logo.svg","key":"voix"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"maintenance-that-works","title":"Maintenance that keeps your voice current","authors":["voix"],"tags":["maintenance","portfolio"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"A practical docs migration playbook","permalink":"/blog/docs-migration-playbook"},"nextItem":{"title":"The six beats of a strong case study","permalink":"/blog/case-study-beats"}},"content":"A portfolio should not drift out of date. The annual care plan keeps your\\ncontent current without a long queue or another subscription.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nWe handle:\\n\\n- New projects and updates\\n- Small copy edits\\n- Publishing support\\n- Lightweight design tweaks\\n\\nYou keep control of the repo, and we keep your voice consistent."},{"id":"case-study-beats","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/case-study-beats","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2025-08-01-mdx-blog-post.mdx","source":"@site/blog/2025-08-01-mdx-blog-post.mdx","title":"The six beats of a strong case study","description":"Great case studies are short and structured. We use six beats so every project","date":"2025-08-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"portfolio","permalink":"/blog/tags/portfolio"},{"inline":true,"label":"process","permalink":"/blog/tags/process"}],"readingTime":0.39,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"voix Studio","title":"Portfolio studio","url":"https://voix.studio","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/voix"},"imageURL":"/img/logo.svg","key":"voix"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"case-study-beats","title":"The six beats of a strong case study","authors":["voix"],"tags":["portfolio","process"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Maintenance that keeps your voice current","permalink":"/blog/maintenance-that-works"},"nextItem":{"title":"The no-license portfolio stack","permalink":"/blog/no-license-portfolio-stack"}},"content":"Great case studies are short and structured. We use six beats so every project\\nreads quickly and still feels human.\\n\\n:::tip Quick rule\\nIf a reader cannot summarize the project in 20 seconds, it is too long.\\n:::\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The six beats\\n\\n1. The context\\n2. The challenge\\n3. Your approach\\n4. The craft or tools\\n5. The outcome\\n6. The takeaway\\n\\nKeep each beat to 1-3 sentences. Add one metric or quote if you have it."},{"id":"no-license-portfolio-stack","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/no-license-portfolio-stack","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2025-05-29-long-blog-post.md","source":"@site/blog/2025-05-29-long-blog-post.md","title":"The no-license portfolio stack","description":"voix sites are built on an open-source stack, which means you never pay","date":"2025-05-29T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"pricing","permalink":"/blog/tags/pricing"},{"inline":true,"label":"process","permalink":"/blog/tags/process"}],"readingTime":0.44,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"voix Studio","title":"Portfolio studio","url":"https://voix.studio","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/voix"},"imageURL":"/img/logo.svg","key":"voix"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"no-license-portfolio-stack","title":"The no-license portfolio stack","authors":["voix"],"tags":["pricing","process"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"The six beats of a strong case study","permalink":"/blog/case-study-beats"},"nextItem":{"title":"Why voice matters more than a template","permalink":"/blog/voice-first-portfolios"}},"content":"voix sites are built on an open-source stack, which means you never pay\\nsoftware license fees to keep your portfolio live.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nHere is what that means in practice:\\n\\n- No monthly per-seat fees\\n- No paywalled publishing tools\\n- No surprise charges when your site grows\\n\\nYou pay for the build once, then an annual care plan if you want ongoing\\nupdates. Hosting on GitHub Pages is free, and custom domains are billed at\\ncost.\\n\\nThis structure keeps your portfolio lean, predictable, and fully in your\\ncontrol."},{"id":"voice-first-portfolios","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/voice-first-portfolios","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2025-05-28-first-blog-post.md","source":"@site/blog/2025-05-28-first-blog-post.md","title":"Why voice matters more than a template","description":"A portfolio should sound like you, not like the last template you downloaded.","date":"2025-05-28T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"voice","permalink":"/blog/tags/voice"},{"inline":true,"label":"portfolio","permalink":"/blog/tags/portfolio"}],"readingTime":0.45,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"voix Studio","title":"Portfolio studio","url":"https://voix.studio","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/voix"},"imageURL":"/img/logo.svg","key":"voix"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"voice-first-portfolios","title":"Why voice matters more than a template","authors":["voix"],"tags":["voice","portfolio"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"The no-license portfolio stack","permalink":"/blog/no-license-portfolio-stack"},"nextItem":{"title":"Information architecture for products that keep growing","permalink":"/blog/information-architecture-for-growing-products"}},"content":"A portfolio should sound like you, not like the last template you downloaded.\\nAt voix, the goal is simple: shape the story first, then design the site\\naround it.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nWhen your voice is clear, clients understand what you do, why it matters, and\\nhow to work with you. That clarity is the difference between a skim and a\\nreply.\\n\\nWe start with a one-line promise, build a short narrative around it, and use\\ncase studies to prove it. Design supports the story, not the other way around."},{"id":"information-architecture-for-growing-products","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/information-architecture-for-growing-products","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2025-04-18-information-architecture-for-growing-products.mdx","source":"@site/blog/2025-04-18-information-architecture-for-growing-products.mdx","title":"Information architecture for products that keep growing","description":"Documentation breaks down when product complexity outgrows the original","date":"2025-04-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Information Architecture","permalink":"/blog/tags/information-architecture","description":"Taxonomy, navigation, and information models for documentation."},{"inline":false,"label":"Docs Architecture","permalink":"/blog/tags/docs-architecture","description":"Platform and system design decisions for scalable documentation."}],"readingTime":0.56,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"voix Studio","title":"Portfolio studio","url":"https://voix.studio","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/voix"},"imageURL":"/img/logo.svg","key":"voix"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"information-architecture-for-growing-products","title":"Information architecture for products that keep growing","authors":["voix"],"tags":["information-architecture","docs-architecture"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Why voice matters more than a template","permalink":"/blog/voice-first-portfolios"},"nextItem":{"title":"Why documentation structure matters more than writing style","permalink":"/blog/why-doc-structure-matters"}},"content":"Documentation breaks down when product complexity outgrows the original\\nnavigation model.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nInformation architecture is how you prevent that breakdown. It defines how\\ncontent is grouped, labeled, linked, and maintained over time.\\n\\n:::tip IA checkpoint\\nIf two teams describe the same feature with different terms, your IA needs a\\ncontrolled vocabulary.\\n:::\\n\\nA scalable IA usually includes:\\n\\n1. A product taxonomy shared across docs, support, and release workflows\\n2. Information types (concept, task, reference, tutorial) with templates\\n3. Metadata standards for audience, lifecycle, and product area\\n4. Governance for ownership, review frequency, and deprecation\\n\\nWhen IA is treated as architecture instead of cleanup work, documentation stays\\ncoherent as teams, APIs, and products evolve."},{"id":"why-doc-structure-matters","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/why-doc-structure-matters","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2024-09-03-why-doc-structure-matters.md","source":"@site/blog/2024-09-03-why-doc-structure-matters.md","title":"Why documentation structure matters more than writing style","description":"Good writing helps. Good structure scales.","date":"2024-09-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Content Structure","permalink":"/blog/tags/content-structure","description":"Patterns that make documentation easier to navigate and maintain."},{"inline":false,"label":"Developer Experience","permalink":"/blog/tags/developer-experience","description":"Documentation practices that improve onboarding and usability."}],"readingTime":0.52,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"voix Studio","title":"Portfolio studio","url":"https://voix.studio","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/voix"},"imageURL":"/img/logo.svg","key":"voix"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"why-doc-structure-matters","title":"Why documentation structure matters more than writing style","authors":["voix"],"tags":["content-structure","developer-experience"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Information architecture for products that keep growing","permalink":"/blog/information-architecture-for-growing-products"},"nextItem":{"title":"How to migrate docs without vendor lock-in","permalink":"/blog/docs-migration-without-lockin"}},"content":"Good writing helps. Good structure scales.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nIf users cannot predict where information lives, even clear writing becomes\\nhard to use. Structure is what makes documentation navigable under pressure.\\n\\nStrong documentation structure gives teams:\\n\\n- Faster answers through consistent page patterns\\n- Better search performance with predictable headings and metadata\\n- Easier maintenance because content types are clearly separated\\n- Fewer contradictions between guides, references, and release notes\\n\\nA practical baseline is simple:\\n\\n- Keep one task per page\\n- Separate concepts, procedures, and API reference content\\n- Use consistent heading depth and naming conventions\\n- Add ownership and review cadence to critical pages\\n\\nStyle improves readability. Structure improves findability and trust."},{"id":"docs-migration-without-lockin","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/docs-migration-without-lockin","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2024-03-12-docs-migration-without-lockin.md","source":"@site/blog/2024-03-12-docs-migration-without-lockin.md","title":"How to migrate docs without vendor lock-in","description":"Moving documentation out of an expensive platform can feel risky, but staying","date":"2024-03-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Docs Migration","permalink":"/blog/tags/docs-migration","description":"Moving documentation from proprietary tools to open-source stacks."},{"inline":false,"label":"Docs as Code","permalink":"/blog/tags/docs-as-code","description":"Git-based documentation workflows with CI/CD and quality checks."}],"readingTime":0.75,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"voix Studio","title":"Portfolio studio","url":"https://voix.studio","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/voix"},"imageURL":"/img/logo.svg","key":"voix"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"docs-migration-without-lockin","title":"How to migrate docs without vendor lock-in","authors":["voix"],"tags":["docs-migration","docs-as-code"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Why documentation structure matters more than writing style","permalink":"/blog/why-doc-structure-matters"}},"content":"Moving documentation out of an expensive platform can feel risky, but staying\\nlocked in is usually more expensive over time.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nThe goal is not just to move pages. The goal is to move to a system you own,\\nwhere content is versioned, testable, and portable.\\n\\nA reliable migration usually follows five steps:\\n\\n1. Inventory your content and identify duplicates, gaps, and stale pages.\\n2. Define the target information model (types, metadata, and templates).\\n3. Convert and normalize content into open formats like Markdown.\\n4. Validate links, embeds, redirects, and navigation before launch.\\n5. Cut over with CI/CD so updates stay safe after migration.\\n\\nThe biggest mistake is treating migration as a copy-and-paste project. It is a\\nplatform change, which means structure and workflows matter as much as content.\\n\\nWhen done well, migration reduces tooling cost, improves collaboration, and\\nmakes your documentation easier to maintain for years."}]}}')}}]);